    publishDate: "2025-12-30",
    excerpt: "Boardroom lighting design must serve multiple functions: bright enough for document work, controlled enough for screen presentations, flattering for video conferencing, and impressive enough to create a premium environmental experience.",
    tags: ["lighting", "boardroom", "design", "technology"],
    internalLinks: [{ anchor: "Boardroom Design Guide", href: "/blog/boardroom-furniture-guide-australia" }, { anchor: "Reception Lighting Guide", href: "/blog/reception-lighting-design-guide" }],
    content: `
<p class="lead">Boardroom lighting must serve multiple functions simultaneously: bright enough for document review and writing, adjustable for presentation mode without blackout, flattering for video conferencing participants, and atmospheric enough to create a premium environmental experience that matches the room's furniture and material quality. Getting this right requires layered lighting design with scene control — and it is one of the investments that most dramatically differentiates premium boardrooms from standard meeting rooms.</p>

<h2>Task Lighting Requirements</h2>
<p>Document review and laptop work at the boardroom table require a minimum of 300 lux at the table surface — ideally 350–400 lux for comfortable fine-detail work. Achieve this through downlights directly above the table (supplemented by pendant lights in premium specifications) set to provide even illumination across the full table length without bright spots or shadowed areas. Task lighting is the baseline scene for working meetings.</p>

<h2>Presentation Mode Lighting</h2>
<p>During screen presentations, ambient lighting should be reducible to a level that improves screen contrast without requiring complete blackout. Target: 100–150 lux ambient in presentation mode, with the table surface illuminated at 200–250 lux to allow note-taking. This requires dimming capability for all ceiling-mounted luminaires — non-dimmable LEDs are inadequate for boardroom specifications. Dimmable LED downlights with dimmer drivers are the standard commercial specification.</p>

<h2>Video Conferencing Lighting</h2>
<p>For hybrid meetings, the lighting quality at participant positions significantly affects how they appear to remote meeting participants. Requirements: adequate frontal illumination of participants' faces (minimum 200 lux from the front), avoidance of strong backlighting that silhouettes faces against windows or feature walls, and colour rendering index (CRI) of 90+ to ensure accurate skin tone reproduction. Dedicated video conferencing lighting — additional fixtures positioned to front-light table occupants — is incorporated in premium boardroom specifications.</p>

<h2>Pendant Lighting as Design Element</h2>
<p>Pendant lights positioned above the boardroom table serve both functional and aesthetic purposes: they provide warm, directed illumination of the table surface independent of ceiling luminaires, create visual interest and perceived ceiling height, and define the table zone within the room. Premium pendant specifications over boardroom tables — sculptural custom fixtures or designer-brand pendants — are a significant design investment with substantial impact on the room's visual quality.</p>

<h2>Scene Control Systems</h2>
<p>Scene-based lighting control is essential for boardroom performance: pre-programmed scenes (Work, Presentation, Video Call, Event) accessible from a wall panel or table-mounted controller make lighting management simple for non-technical users. Integration with the AV control system (automatic scene switching when the display is activated, or when the video conferencing system starts) reduces the cognitive load of managing the meeting environment and ensures correct lighting without manual intervention.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Create a Premium Boardroom Environment</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk designs complete boardroom environments with expert furniture and design guidance. Book a consultation today.</p>
</div>
`,
