    content: `
<p class="lead">Modular meeting room furniture allows a single room to serve multiple meeting formats — a fixed-configuration boardroom can only host one type of meeting. In commercial environments where every square metre carries significant rental cost, modular furniture that allows the same room to serve a 12-person board meeting, a 20-person workshop, and a 40-person town hall event maximises asset utilisation and can justify a significantly larger room investment.</p>

<h2>The Case for Modular Flexibility</h2>
<p>Typical commercial meeting room utilisation in Australian offices runs at 40–60% across the working day. Rooms that can serve only one configuration are idle during the majority of periods when a different format would be useful. Modular systems that allow rapid reconfiguration increase effective utilisation significantly — a room that serves board meetings in the morning, a workshop in the afternoon, and an all-hands session in the evening generates far more value per square metre than a fixed-configuration equivalent.</p>

<h2>Modular Table Systems</h2>
<p>The commercial furniture market offers purpose-designed modular table systems in several configurations:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nesting tables:</strong> Individual tables that nest together for storage when not in use. Quickly configured in rows, U-shape, cluster, or boardroom layout. Most versatile option for true multi-use rooms.</li>
<li><strong>Folding leg tables:</strong> Tables with folding legs that collapse for vertical storage on trolleys. Lighter weight and easier to move than rigid-frame tables.</li>
<li><strong>Modular panel tables:</strong> Tables with modular top panels and shared leg systems that combine to create larger tables or separate into individual units. Better aesthetic quality than nesting tables; slightly less flexible.</li>
<li><strong>Fixed-split tables:</strong> Large boardroom tables designed to split into sections — boardroom configuration for board meetings, separated for training. Provides a premium fixed look that disassembles.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Chair Flexibility</h2>
<p>Multi-use rooms require chair systems that can adapt with the table configuration: stacking chairs for storage during alternative configurations, tablet-arm chairs for training and workshop formats, and quality conference chairs for boardroom mode. Specifying multiple chair types and storing non-active configurations in purpose-designed storage trolleys within the room enables rapid changeover.</p>

<h2>Storage Within the Room</h2>
<p>Modular furniture requires storage for non-configured elements. Design the room with: a built-in storage alcove or joinery wall that accommodates folded tables and stacked chairs, purpose-designed trolleys for table transport and storage, and clear labelling of configurations for quick set-up by non-specialist staff. Without dedicated storage, modular furniture accumulates in corners and corridors, defeating its purpose.</p>

<h2>Quality vs. Flexibility Trade-off</h2>
<p>The practical trade-off of modular systems is aesthetic — modular tables rarely achieve the visual quality of a bespoke fixed boardroom table. For rooms that serve both senior executive and general meeting purposes, two solutions apply: configure the modular system for the boardroom mode and accept the visual compromise, or specify a premium modular system (Bene, Haworth, or similar) where the modular structure is of sufficient quality for boardroom-level presentation.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Design Flexible Meeting Spaces</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk supplies modular meeting room solutions for Australian offices. Request a quote today.</p>
</div>
`,
