    content: `
<p class="lead">Australian boardroom design is evolving rapidly. The dark timber, leather, and imposing formality of the traditional corporate boardroom is giving way to contemporary environments that project authority through material quality and precision rather than historical convention — spaces that feel as modern as the businesses they house and as impressive as the decisions made within them. Understanding current trends helps organisations make boardroom investments that will remain relevant through the full lease cycle.</p>

<h2>Trend 1: Lighter Material Palettes</h2>
<p>The shift from dark timber to lighter, more varied material palettes is the most visible trend in Australian corporate boardroom design. White oak, American ash, and Tasmanian oak veneers are replacing the mahogany and walnut that dominated boardroom specifications for decades. Stone surfaces (marble, granite, and engineered stone) appear on table tops and feature wall applications. The effect is a boardroom that feels simultaneously more contemporary and more honest about material quality.</p>

<h2>Trend 2: Biophilic Integration</h2>
<p>Living walls, feature plant installations, and natural material integration are appearing in premium boardroom specifications across Australian markets. Research-backed wellness design has moved from open-plan floors into meeting rooms, creating boardrooms where the board member experience includes the wellbeing benefits of natural connection. A feature plant wall visible from all seats, or a large-scale living moss installation on the feature wall, creates a distinctive environment impossible to replicate in conventional boardrooms.</p>

<h2>Trend 3: Technology Invisibility</h2>
<p>The most sophisticated boardroom technology systems are designed to be invisible — screens that retract into credenza cabinetry when not in use, microphones concealed in ceiling and furniture, cable management that leaves no surface wire visible, and AV panels that are flush with the wall surface. The trend away from visible technology equipment reflects a growing preference for environments that look designed rather than equipped.</p>

<h2>Trend 4: Warm Metal Accents</h2>
<p>Brushed brass, warm gold, and bronze tones are replacing the cool chrome and brushed stainless steel that dominated specification choices a decade ago. These warmer metals appear in light fixture fittings, door hardware, credenza handles, table edge profiles, and decorative screen elements. The warmth of these metals complements the lighter timber and stone palettes that characterise contemporary boardroom material selections.</p>

<h2>Trend 5: Sustainability Credentials</h2>
<p>Premium clients and board members increasingly notice and value environmental credentials in business environments. FSC-certified timber, recycled content panel products, low-VOC materials, and locally manufactured furniture are appearing as specified requirements in high-profile boardroom fitouts. WELL Building Standard boardroom criteria — air quality, lighting, thermal comfort, and material health — are increasingly referenced in brief documents for premium fitouts.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Design a Contemporary Boardroom</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk creates boardroom environments that reflect current design thinking. Book a consultation with our design team.</p>
</div>
`,
