    content: `
<p class="lead">The boardroom credenza is the functional workhorse of the executive meeting room — providing storage, AV equipment housing, refreshment service surface, technology equipment concealment, and visual grounding for the room's primary wall — yet it is consistently treated as an afterthought in boardroom design. Specifying the credenza with the same care as the table and chairs creates a complete, coherent room environment and prevents the functional and aesthetic compromises that afterthought credenzas create.</p>

<h2>Functions of the Boardroom Credenza</h2>
<p>A well-specified boardroom credenza serves multiple simultaneous functions:</p>
<ul>
<li>AV equipment housing — display screen stand, video conferencing codec, cable management, and signal routing equipment</li>
<li>Meeting room technology management — wireless presentation system, room booking terminal, and AV control system</li>
<li>Refreshment service — surface area for coffee, water, and catering that is appropriately scaled and positioned for service during meetings</li>
<li>General storage — stationery, HDMI cables, remotes, spare technology, and meeting room supplies</li>
<li>Visual composition — balancing the wall plane opposite the boardroom table with an appropriate mass of furniture</li>
</ul>

<h2>Dimensional Guidance</h2>
<p>Credenza dimensions for a standard boardroom: 1800–2400mm length (proportioned to the room width), 500–600mm depth, 900–1000mm height (working surface height allowing standing use). Taller credenzas (1200mm+) with open shelving or display space above the base cabinet serve rooms where additional wall presence is desired. Credenzas positioned below wall-mounted screens should be set at a height that positions the screen centre at eye level when seated.</p>

<h2>AV Integration in the Credenza</h2>
<p>AV equipment integration requires: ventilated internal compartments for heat-generating equipment (AV codec, switching, and signal distribution generate significant heat requiring active ventilation), cable management apertures for cable entry from the floor and exit to wall-mounted screens, dedicated power outlets at equipment height within the cabinet, and access doors on both sides of the credenza for servicing from front and back.</p>

<h2>Material Coordination</h2>
<p>The credenza should share the material palette of the boardroom table — same timber species, same lacquer colour, or coordinating material — creating a coherent design language across the room's furniture. Mismatched materials between table and credenza create a visual discontinuity that undermines the room's designed quality. Where a different material is intentionally used (stone credenza top with timber table), the relationship should be deliberate rather than accidental.</p>

<div class="cta-block">
<h3>Complete Your Boardroom Specification</h3>
<p>The Corporate Desk designs and supplies complete boardroom furniture including tables, chairs, and credenzas. Request a quote.</p>
</div>
`,
